  "Question": "In \\(y=A\\sin(\\omega x+\\varphi)\\), what is the amplitude?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "Always \\(A\\) itself",
    "B": "\\(\\omega\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\varphi\\)",
    "D": "The absolute value of \\(A\\)"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "Assuming \\(\\omega\\ne0\\), as real \\(x\\) varies, the angle \\(\\omega x+\\varphi\\) takes every real value. Since sine takes every value in \\([-1,1]\\), the range of \\(y\\) is \\([-|A|,|A|]\\), so its amplitude is \\(|A|\\), choice D. When \\(A\\) is negative, the graph is flipped vertically, and the amplitude is still the absolute value.\n\nThe question does not exclude \\(\\omega=0\\). In that case \\(y=A\\sin\\varphi\\) is constant and has zero oscillation amplitude. For example, \\(A=2\\), \\(\\omega=0\\), and \\(\\varphi=0\\) give \\(y=0\\), whose amplitude is \\(0\\) although \\(|A|=2\\). Thus D is justified under the nonzero-frequency assumption, but the unqualified question should not be graded exclusively as D.",
